\ ember.fr — the self-hosted ember: an interactive, visual single-stepper for fr,
\ written in fr. Run it with no Python at all — just give fr the source files and
\ type a stepping command at the prompt (raw-mode key input needs a real tty, which
\ a terminal provides):
\
\     ./fr prelude.fr term.fr ember.fr        then type:  5 ' square ember
\
\ The `ember-fr` script is a convenience/test wrapper around exactly that (it adds a
\ pty so keystrokes can be scripted, and pre-types the command):
\
\     ./ember-fr "5 ' square ember"      \ step `square` with 5 on the stack
\     ./ember-fr --selftest              \ headless pty check
\
\ This is the fr-native analog of the Python `ember`. Where that one ptraces the
\ real engine and draws seven panels, this steps a word's threaded body on the live
\ data stack and paints it with term.fr. All three sides are now self-hosted: the
\ model (see/trace), the input (key/raw-on), the output (term.fr). This file is just
\ the loop that ties them together.
\
\   keys  : space / s = step one cell   ·   r = run to the end   ·   q / Esc = quit
\   panels: call (the call path, e.g. cube > square) · code (the current word's body,
\           current cell highlighted) · data (the live stack).
\   scope : follows control flow — if/else/then and begin/until/while/repeat step
\           (0branch pops the live flag; branch moves the cursor), and it STEPS INTO
\           colon words (it descends through docol/EXIT, tracking its own call stack),
\           just like the Python ember hopping into a definition. Primitives are
\           atomic (machine code). The code panel decodes linearly like `see`, so on a
\           backward jump the cursor hops up; cells it has passed are shown faint.
\
\ What stays in the Python `ember` (out of fr's reach): the ptrace backend that
\ single-steps the *real* machine instructions, and its richer curses view (RETURN
\ frames, dictionary, Nord palette). Depends on prelude.fr + term.fr.

\ --- state -------------------------------------------------------------------
variable ecfa        \ CFA of the top-level word being stepped (the call root)
variable ecur        \ CFA of the word we're *currently inside* (innermost frame)
variable ebp         \ the step cursor: the next cell to execute
variable dbp         \ scratch cursor used while drawing the thread
variable edone       \ -1 once the top-level word has returned
variable equit       \ -1 once the user has asked to quit
variable estepn      \ how many cells we've executed so far

\ Call stack: two parallel stacks pushed together on each descent into a colon
\ word, popped together on EXIT. rstk = where to resume in the caller; cstk = the
\ caller's word (so we can restore the title/thread and draw the RETURN panel).
variable rstk  64 cells allot     variable rn
variable cstk  64 cells allot     variable cn
: rpush ( a -- )  rstk rn @ cells + !  rn @ 1+ rn ! ;
: rpop  ( -- a )  rn @ 1- rn !  rstk rn @ cells + @ ;
: cpush ( a -- )  cstk cn @ cells + !  cn @ 1+ cn ! ;
: cpop  ( -- a )  cn @ 1- cn !  cstk cn @ cells + @ ;

\ --- the stepper -------------------------------------------------------------
\ estep runs the one cell at ebp on the live data stack and advances ebp,
\ mirroring the kernel's inner interpreter by moving ebp (it can't `execute` a
\ branch/EXIT without clobbering its own IP). It *steps into colon words*, just
\ like the Python ember hopping through docol/EXIT:
\   EXIT     -> pop a call frame (resume the caller); at the root, we're done.
\   LIT n    -> push n, skip the inline value.
\   BRANCH   -> ebp += inline offset (relative to the offset cell, as in fr.s).
\   0BRANCH  -> pop the live flag; jump if it's 0, else fall through (cf. ZBRANCH).
\   a colon word (codeword = docol) -> DESCEND: push a frame, ebp = its body.
\   a primitive -> `execute` it (it's machine code; atomic), then step one cell.
: estep ( -- )
  ebp @ @                                                    ( cfa )
  dup 'exit = if  drop                              \ return from the current word
    rn @ 0= if  -1 edone !  else  cpop ecur !  rpop ebp !  then               exit  then
  dup 'lit     = if  drop  ebp @ 8 + @         ebp @ 16 + ebp !  estepn @ 1+ estepn !  exit  then
  dup 'branch  = if  drop  ebp @ 8 + dup @ +   ebp !            estepn @ 1+ estepn !  exit  then
  dup '0branch = if  drop                          \ the flag is live on the data stack
    0= if  ebp @ 8 + dup @ +  ebp !  else  ebp @ 16 + ebp !  then
    estepn @ 1+ estepn !                                                    exit  then
  dup @ 'docol = if                                \ a colon word: step into it
    ebp @ 8 +  rpush                               \   resume after this cell in the caller
    ecur @     cpush                               \   remember the caller word
    dup  ecur !                                    \   we're now inside the callee
    8 +  ebp !                                     \   ebp = callee body (cfa + 8)
  else
    execute  ebp @ 8 + ebp !                       \ a primitive: run it, step on
  then
  estepn @ 1+ estepn ! ;

\ erun — step until the top-level word returns (run-to-end / autoplay).
: erun ( -- )  begin  edone @ 0=  while  estep  repeat ;

\ einit — set up to step <word> with its CFA on top (args already beneath it).
: einit ( cfa -- )
  dup ecfa !  dup ecur !  cell+ ebp !
  0 edone !  0 equit !  0 estepn !  0 rn !  0 cn ! ;

\ --- short text labels (fr has no string literals, so we spell them) ---------
: .ember ( -- ) [char] e emit [char] m emit [char] b emit [char] e emit [char] r emit ;
: .code  ( -- ) [char] c emit [char] o emit [char] d emit [char] e emit ;
: .data  ( -- ) [char] d emit [char] a emit [char] t emit [char] a emit ;
: .call  ( -- ) [char] c emit [char] a emit [char] l emit [char] l emit ;
: .stepk ( -- ) [char] s emit [char] p emit [char] a emit [char] c emit [char] e emit
                [char] = emit [char] s emit [char] t emit [char] e emit [char] p emit ;
: .runk  ( -- ) [char] r emit [char] = emit [char] r emit [char] u emit [char] n emit ;
: .quitk ( -- ) [char] q emit [char] = emit [char] q emit [char] u emit [char] i emit [char] t emit ;
: .done  ( -- ) [char] d emit [char] o emit [char] n emit [char] e emit ;

\ --- drawing -----------------------------------------------------------------
\ The thread panel walks the body like `see`, colouring each cell by position:
\ executed (before ebp) faint, the next cell (at ebp) bold yellow, upcoming plain.
: tok-colour ( -- )
  dbp @ ebp @ = if  1 sgr 3 fg   exit then    \ current : bold yellow
  dbp @ ebp @ < if  2 sgr        exit then    \ executed: faint
  reset ;                                       \ upcoming: normal
: draw-tok ( -- )                 \ decode + print the cell at dbp, advance dbp
  tok-colour
  dbp @ @
  dup 'exit    = if  drop [char] ; emit                                        dbp @ 8 +  dbp !
  else dup 'lit    = if  drop [char] # emit  dbp @ 8 + @ .n                    dbp @ 16 + dbp !
  else dup 'branch = if  drop [char] b emit space dbp @ 8 + @ .n              dbp @ 16 + dbp !
  else dup '0branch = if  drop [char] 0 emit [char] b emit space dbp @ 8 + @ .n  dbp @ 16 + dbp !
  else  .cfaname                                                              dbp @ 8 +  dbp !
  then then then then
  reset space ;
: draw-thread ( -- )              \ decode the *current* word's body, highlighting ebp
  ecur @ cell+ dbp !
  begin  dbp @ @ 'exit =  >r  draw-tok  r>  until ;

\ The call path: the enclosing words (cstk, outermost first) then the current one,
\ e.g.  cube > square .  This is ember.fr's RETURN panel.
variable ci
: draw-call ( -- )
  0 ci !
  begin  ci @ cn @ <  while
    cstk ci @ cells + @  .cfaname  space [char] > emit space
    ci @ 1+ ci !
  repeat
  1 sgr  ecur @ .cfaname  reset ;     \ innermost (current) word, bold

: draw-status ( -- )
  edone @ if  2 sgr .done reset  else  .stepk  space space .runk  then
  space space  .quitk ;

: draw ( -- )                     \ one full frame (clear + repaint)
  clear
  1 2 at  6 fg bold  .ember  [char] : emit  space  ecfa @ .cfaname  reset
          space space  [char] # emit  estepn @ .n
  3 2 at  2 sgr .call reset    3 9 at  draw-call
  5 2 at  2 sgr .code reset    5 9 at  draw-thread
  7 2 at  2 sgr .data reset    7 9 at  .s
  9 2 at  draw-status ;

\ --- the event loop ----------------------------------------------------------
: ember ( cfa -- )                \ usage:  <args> ' <word> ember
  einit
  raw-on  hide-cursor
  begin
    draw
    key                                            ( c )
    dup 0<              if  drop  -1 equit !
    else dup [char] q = if  drop  -1 equit !
    else dup 27 =       if  drop  -1 equit !        \ Esc
    else dup [char] r = if  drop  erun              \ run to the end
    else dup [char] s = over 32 = or if  drop  edone @ 0= if estep then
    else drop
    then then then then then
    equit @
  until
  show-cursor  reset  raw-off  cr  bye ;
